<Base title="Affiliate Disclosure | Sundays with Anna" description="How Sundays with Anna uses affiliate links and handles gifted products and partnerships.">
<section class="p" style="padding-top:56px;max-width:900px;margin:0 auto"><div class="up" style="color:#8A8A8A">Sundays with Anna</div><h1 class="hd" style="margin:10px 0 8px;font-size:84px;line-height:.95">Affiliate disclosure</h1><div class="hand" style="font-size:34px;transform:rotate(-2deg)">honest, always</div></section>
<section class="p" style="padding-top:40px;padding-bottom:40px"><div class="art-body" style="max-width:760px;margin:0 auto">
<p><em>Last updated: October 25, 2026</em></p>
<p>Some links on this site may be <strong>affiliate links</strong>. If you click one and make a purchase, I may earn a small commission at no extra cost to you. This helps me keep creating free content.</p>
<h2>My rules</h2>
<ul class="chk">
<li>I only recommend products I have used myself or would use.</li>
<li>Commissions never decide what I say about a product.</li>
<li>When a link is an affiliate link, I say so near the link or on the page.</li>
<li>When a product was gifted or part of a partnership, I say so in the review.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Amazon</h2>
<p>If I join the Amazon Associates program, I will add this statement: “As an Amazon Associate I earn from qualifying purchases.”</p>
<h2>Why I tell you</h2>
<p>In the United States, the Federal Trade Commission asks creators to clearly disclose material connections with brands. I want you to trust what you read here, so I keep it simple and visible.</p>
<p>Questions? Visit the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>.</p>
</div></section>
</Base>
